  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Periods of rain tonight through Friday, along with \ngusty winds. \n\nSharp mid level trough will advance into the Great Lakes tonight \nthrough Friday. Surface low pressure will strengthen across the \nGreat lakes tonight and move into southern Ontario by Friday \nmorning, reaching the Saint Lawrence Valley by Friday afternoon.\nThe expected track of the surface low will allow the warm sector\nto build into the lower Great Lakes. A period of strong warm \nadvection and moisture transport, enhanced by a 50+ knot low level\njet, will arrive tonight and support an initial wave of rainfall.\nThis rain will arrive in western New York by around midnight, \nthen spread quickly northeast overnight. Additional periods of \nrain will continue Friday through Friday evening as the trailing\ncold front moves southeast across the region, supported by mid \nlevel height falls and right entrance region jet dynamics. \nMarginal instability in the warm sector may support a few \nembedded thunderstorms as well late tonight through Friday.\n\nRainfall amounts will vary widely across the area, generally ranging \nfrom 0.25\" to 1.0\". Terrain will play an important role given the \nstrong boundary layer flow, with downslope areas of the Lake Erie \nshore and Genesee Valley likely on the low end of the range, while \nhigher terrain is more likely to be on the higher end of the range \nfrom upslope enhancement. There may be a few small areas that exceed \n1.0\" if embedded convection develops.\n\nIt will become quite windy late tonight through Friday. A 50+ knot\nlow level jet will propagate across the eastern Great Lakes late\ntonight through Friday morning. Gusts will increase into the \n25-35 mph range areawide, with gusts possibly reaching or \nexceeding 40 mph in downslope areas along the Lake Erie shore \nand north of the Tug Hill Plateau briefly. Boundary layer flow \nwill quickly swing around to the southwest Friday morning \nthrough mid afternoon, with gusts still in the 25-35 mph range \nin most areas, and up to 45 mph northeast of Lake Erie across \nthe Niagara Frontier.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Much colder Friday night through Saturday night with \na few wet snowflakes possible early Saturday morning.\n\nStrong cold advection will develop Friday night in the wake of the \ndeparting low, with 850 mb temperatures dropping into the -5C to \n-8C range by Saturday morning. The cold air aloft will support \nincreasing lake induced instability overnight with a brief lake \nresponse, but short NNW fetch and a rapidly drying synoptic scale \nairmass will keep lake effect muted and brief. Lake effect and \nupslope rain showers will develop southeast of the lakes Friday \nnight, with a few wet snowflakes possibly mixing in very late Friday \nnight through early Saturday morning before the lake effect showers \nquickly end by late morning. The best chance of a few snowflakes \nwill be across higher terrain, but no accumulation is expected.\n\nThe coldest airmass of the fall season so far will arrive for \nSaturday, with highs only in the 40s areawide, and even upper 30s \nacross the highest terrain despite some afternoon sunshine. \nTemperatures will drop back into the low to mid 30s Saturday night \non the lake plains, with some 20s likely across the Southern Tier \nvalleys and North Country. The cold air will quickly depart, with \ntemperatures back above average by early next week.",
